  but they instead I told them look I have  a I have a problem I need help and they  sent me to a rehab so  long story short I left that rehab  relapsed again and then got locked up  they gave me a second chance went back  to rehab finished the program graduated  got off probation  got a regular job one of my first  regular paying jobs and I just started  working the regular nine to five and  then met my wife and  a lot of things have changed  what do you what do you credit that  those changes to  while I was in rehab and  just going through the months and I  slowly started to just have things of my  own I got my license I saved up for a  car I had my own clothes and they were  telling me about an apartment program so  I was like wow so as soon as I started  getting these things that I could call  my own it motivated me to see how far I  could go with it eventually got my  apartment and of course  I still relapse after I got clean but I  feel like that's a part of recovery and  as long as you don't slip back into it  that's all that matters  but it was was your therapy involved or  anything like that  no so there was not much therapy  involved um you had a lot of emotional  baggage to emotional baggage for sure so  that's where  so the rehab that I went to they were  very work-centric and they would they  had us doing a lot of Labor and keeping  us busy  um and I'm not sure that is exactly what  I needed but it worked and I've pretty  much learned how to hold down a stable  job and work hard and be grateful for  what I had  but  I want to say  I was really just sick and tired of  being on the streets and I was really  relieved to just have finally found  just the normal life that I could live  and just tried to stick to it my oh I  cannot forget to give my mom some credit  because she's the one that showed up to  every court date she continued to  support me through rehab and without her  I don't know where I would be for sure  that's usually what what it takes is  